    tags: [
      "treadmill lubrication",
      "treadmill belt",
      "silicone lubricant",
      "treadmill maintenance",
      "home gym",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-lube" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-lube)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Owner's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          How to <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">Lubricate</span> a Treadmill Belt
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Ten minutes, twice a year, one ounce of silicone. It is the single
          highest-return job in home fitness equipment, and skipping it is the
          most common reason treadmills die at four years old instead of
          twelve.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#steps" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Jump to the Steps
          </a>
          <a href="#what-not-to-use" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            What Not to Use
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">September 3, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="6.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-red-900">Two things before you start.</strong>
          Unplug the machine at the wall — your hands go under a belt that a
          knocked console button could start. And check your manual: a minority
          of decks are pre-waxed or self-lubricating, and applying silicone to
          those makes them worse. Everything below assumes a conventional deck
          that takes silicone.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-it-matters" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. What Lubrication Actually Does</a>
          <a href="#how-often" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. How Often, and How to Tell</a>
          <a href="#what-to-use" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. What to Use, and How Much</a>
          <a href="#what-not-to-use" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. What Not to Use, Ever</a>
          <a href="#steps" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. The Eight-Step Method</a>
          <a href="#waxed" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Pre-Waxed and Self-Lubricating Decks</a>
          <a href="#after" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. What to Expect Afterwards</a>
          <a href="#went-wrong" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. If Something Feels Wrong After</a>
          <a href="#too-late" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Is It Too Late for My Machine?</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Almost nobody does this, and almost everybody should. Deck
          lubrication is the maintenance job with the largest gap between how
          trivial it is and how much damage skipping it does — and because a
          dry deck produces no symptom for two or three years, the owner has no
          idea anything is wrong until the repair quote arrives.
        </p>

        <div id="why-it-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. What Lubrication Actually Does</h2>
          <p>
            A treadmill belt does not roll across the deck. It slides. The
            rollers at each end carry it around, but along the whole length of
            the running surface the underside of the belt is dragging directly
            across a fixed board, with your body weight pressing the two
            together. The silicone sits in that interface and is the only thing
            stopping it from being a friction brake.
          </p>
          <p>
            When it depletes, the effect is not felt underfoot for a long time.
            What happens instead is electrical: the controller senses the belt
            slowing under the increased drag and feeds the motor more current to
            hold the set speed. More current produces more heat, in the motor
            windings and in the speed controller board. Neither has anywhere much
            to dump that heat, so both run hotter than designed, month after
            month.
          </p>
          <p>
            Electronics fail from cumulative thermal stress rather than from a
            single bad day, which is why the eventual failure looks unrelated to
            the cause. A four-year-old machine with a dead controller board was
            usually killed by a deck that ran dry in year two. The owner
            reasonably concludes the treadmill was poorly made.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is a second, cheaper consequence worth naming: a dry deck wears
            the belt itself. Belts are consumable, but a belt on a lubricated deck
            lasts years and a belt grinding on a dry board can be worn through in
            months, which is why the underside sometimes looks polished or feels
            rough when you finally check it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-often" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. How Often, and How to Tell</h2>
          <p>
            The default interval for normal home use — three to five sessions a
            week — is every three to six months. If you prefer distance,
            somewhere between 150 and 200 miles is the usual guidance. Halve the
            interval if the machine runs several hours a day, or lives on carpet
            or in a garage where dust loading is higher.
          </p>
          <p>
            The physical check takes ten seconds and beats any schedule. Unplug
            the machine, lift the belt at the centre of the deck, and run a
            finger along the deck surface underneath. A lubricated deck feels
            faintly slick, like a waxed surface. A dry deck feels like bare
            board, and if your finger comes back with dark residue on it, the
            belt has been wearing against the deck for a while.
          </p>
          <p>
            The behavioural signs, in the order they usually appear: the machine
            feels like it is dragging slightly at low speed; the motor note is
            higher than you remember at the same speed; the belt hesitates for an
            instant when you plant your weight on one foot. That last one is
            frequently misdiagnosed as a loose belt and "fixed" by over-tightening
            it, which makes the underlying friction problem worse. If a belt is
            slipping, lubricate before you tension — our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt slipping guide</a>
            works through the distinction properly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What to Use, and How Much</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>100% silicone.</strong> That is the whole answer. It is sold
            as treadmill lubricant in squeeze bottles, usually with a long thin
            applicator nozzle that lets you reach the centre of the deck without
            lifting the belt far. Most manufacturers supply a bottle in the box
            and sell refills; generic 100% silicone treadmill lubricant is
            equivalent and usually cheaper. Both liquid and spray forms work,
            though liquid gives you far better control over placement.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>About half an ounce per side.</strong> Roughly one ounce in
            total for a standard 20 by 60-inch deck. That is a much smaller
            quantity than most first-timers expect — it looks like an inadequate
            amount as you apply it, and it is not. The belt distributes it across
            the whole contact area within a few minutes of walking, and the film
            you need is microscopically thin.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Placement matters more than quantity.</strong> Apply along
            the centre line of the deck, in a line or a loose S shape, reaching
            in toward the middle third. The centre is where your feet land and
            where the friction is; silicone applied near the edges migrates off
            the side of the deck and does nothing except mark your floor.
          </p>
          <p>
            One bottle typically covers two to four years of a home machine, so
            the running cost of doing this properly is trivial. There is no
            premium version worth paying for: silicone is silicone, and the
            expensive branded bottles differ mainly in the applicator.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-not-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. What Not to Use, Ever</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>WD-40 and penetrating oils.</strong> WD-40 is a
            water-displacing penetrant, not a lubricant for a sliding polymer
            interface. It attacks the belt backing, softens deck laminates, leaves
            a residue that grabs dust, and is flammable in an enclosure that
            contains a warm motor. It is the most common wrong answer and it is
            comprehensively wrong.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Petroleum oils, motor oil, grease.</strong> Same failure mode.
            Petroleum products degrade the materials the belt and deck are made
            of, and the damage is not reversible — a belt that has been oiled
            needs replacing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cooking sprays and household polish.</strong> Cooking oils
            oxidise into a sticky varnish that increases friction dramatically
            after a few weeks. Furniture polish contains solvents and waxes that
            do not belong under load. Both come up in owner forums and both cause
            more damage than doing nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Silicone sprays that are not 100% silicone.</strong> This is
            the trap that catches careful people. Many aerosol "silicone lubricant"
            products are silicone in a petroleum carrier, and the carrier is the
            problem. Read the label; if it does not say 100% silicone, do not put
            it under your belt.
          </p>
          <p>
            Any of these will also void a warranty the moment a technician opens
            the machine, because the residue is unmistakable. If you have already
            used one of them, see section eight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="steps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The Eight-Step Method</h2>
          <p>
            Ten minutes, a bottle of silicone, and the hex key that came with the
            machine. Nothing else.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-8 grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
        {
          STEPS.map((step) => (
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-5">
              <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
                <span class="flex-shrink-0 w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white font-black text-sm flex items-center justify-center">{step.n}</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1 leading-tight">{step.title}</p>
                  <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed m-0">{step.detail}</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          A note on step three. You do not always need to slacken the belt — on
          many machines there is enough give to lift an edge at the centre
          without touching the bolts. Only back them off if you are having to
          force it, and if you do, count the turns so you can restore the same
          tension afterwards. Getting tension wrong is the most likely way to
          turn a lubrication job into a tracking problem.
        </p>
        <p>
          On step seven, walking the belt in genuinely matters. Silicone applied
          and left sits in two stripes and does nothing until it is spread.
          Three to five minutes at 2 mph distributes it across the full contact
          area, and the machine will feel noticeably lighter by the end of that
          walk if the deck was dry.
        </p>

        <div id="waxed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Pre-Waxed and Self-Lubricating Decks</h2>
          <p>
            A minority of treadmills use decks impregnated with a wax or a
            low-friction laminate that releases lubricant as it heats. These are
            marketed as maintenance-free, and for lubrication purposes they broadly
            are — until someone applies silicone to one.
          </p>
          <p>
            Silicone on a waxed deck does not blend with the wax. It forms a
            slurry that increases friction rather than reducing it, and on some
            decks it interferes with the wax's ability to migrate to the surface,
            which is the mechanism the whole design depends on. The result is a
            machine that gets worse after maintenance, which is a confusing thing
            to diagnose.
          </p>
          <p>
            So check first, every time, on a machine you have not lubricated
            before. The manual will state it plainly. If the manual is long gone,
            the manufacturer's support line will confirm the deck type from the
            model number in under a minute, and that call is worth making before
            you open a bottle.
          </p>
          <p>
            Maintenance-free decks are not eternal. The wax does deplete over
            years, and when it does, the deck is usually replaced rather than
            re-treated. Everything else on this site's
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">maintenance schedule</a>
            — tension, tracking, dust, bolts — applies to these machines
            unchanged.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="after" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. What to Expect Afterwards</h2>
          <p>
            If the deck was genuinely dry, the change is immediate and obvious.
            The motor note drops, the belt feels like it is moving rather than
            being dragged, and any hesitation under foot at low speed disappears
            within the first minute of the walk-in. Several owners describe it as
            the machine feeling new, which is a fair description of removing two
            years of accumulated drag.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the deck was in reasonable condition, you may notice nothing at
            all, and that is the correct outcome. Lubrication is preventive.
            Feeling no difference means you did it before the friction had time to
            climb, which is exactly the intent of a schedule.
          </p>
          <p>
            For the first session or two the belt may feel very slightly slick
            underfoot. That settles as the film thins to its working thickness.
            What you should not see is silicone at the edges of the deck, on the
            side rails or on the floor — if you do, you used too much, and the
            fix is to wipe it up and use less next time rather than to run the
            machine and let it fling around.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="went-wrong" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. If Something Feels Wrong After</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>The belt now drifts off-centre.</strong> Almost always uneven
            application, or unequal tension after restoring the bolts. Re-centre
            it with a quarter turn on the rear bolt on the side it drifts toward,
            waiting thirty seconds between adjustments. Full method in our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">tracking guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The belt now slips under load.</strong> Usually too much
            silicone reducing grip against the front roller, or a belt left too
            slack after step three. Wipe the excess from the roller area with a
            dry cloth, then add a quarter turn of tension to each rear bolt
            equally and re-test.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>It feels worse, not better.</strong> The most likely
            explanation is a pre-waxed deck that should not have been lubricated.
            Stop, confirm the deck type, and contact the manufacturer — several
            will advise on cleaning the deck back before it does harm.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>You already used WD-40 or an oil.</strong> Do not simply add
            silicone on top. Wipe out as much residue as you can reach with a dry
            cloth, then call the manufacturer or a technician: depending on how
            much was used and how long the machine has run since, the belt may
            need replacing. It is a recoverable mistake if caught early and an
            expensive one if ignored.
          </p>
          <p>
            For symptoms unrelated to lubrication — the machine not starting,
            cutting out mid-session, or showing an error code — work through the
            <a href="/treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">troubleshooting guide</a>
            instead.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="too-late" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Is It Too Late for My Machine?</h2>
          <p>
            If you have owned a treadmill for three years and never lubricated it
            — which describes a very large share of owners — the honest answer is
            that it is worth doing today and it may not save you.
          </p>
          <p>
            Do it anyway. Lubricating a long-dry deck removes the ongoing thermal
            stress immediately, and any electronics that have survived so far
            stop accumulating further damage from that source. Machines respond
            well to this: the drag disappears, the motor runs cooler, and plenty
            go on for years afterwards.
          </p>
          <p>
            What lubrication cannot do is undo wear that has already happened. If
            the belt underside is visibly worn, polished or shedding, it needs
            replacing regardless. If the deck board has grooves worn into it,
            lubricant will help but the surface is compromised. And if the
            controller board has been running hot for two years, it may fail next
            month whatever you do now.
          </p>
          <p>
            Whether that failure is worth repairing depends on the machine's age
            and value, which we work through in
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmills last</a>.
            The short version: on a mid-range machine past five years, a
            controller or motor replacement is usually close enough to the price
            of a better new treadmill that replacement wins.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you lubricate a treadmill belt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Every three to six months for normal home use of three to five
                sessions a week, or roughly every 150 to 200 miles if you prefer
                to track it that way. Halve the interval for a machine running
                several hours a day or living in a dusty garage. Always defer to
                your manual, since intervals vary by deck construction.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much silicone should you use on a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                About half an ounce per side, so roughly an ounce in total for a
                standard 20 by 60-inch deck. That is far less than most people
                expect. Over-application is the common error: the excess runs
                off, marks the floor, gets flung into the motor housing and
                attracts dust that forms an abrasive paste under the belt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use WD-40 on a treadmill belt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. WD-40 is a petroleum-based penetrant, not a lubricant for
                this application. It attacks the belt backing and the deck
                laminate, leaves a residue that attracts dust, is flammable near
                a warm motor, and voids warranties on sight. Only 100% silicone
                belongs under a treadmill belt.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you know if a treadmill belt needs lubricating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Unplug the machine, reach under the belt at the centre of the
                deck and run a finger along the surface. A lubricated deck feels
                slightly slick; a dry one feels like bare board. The behavioural
                signs are a belt that hesitates under your foot at low speed, a
                motor note that rises over the months, or a machine that feels
                like it is dragging.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What happens if you over-lubricate a treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Excess silicone runs off the deck onto the floor and the side
                rails, creating a slip hazard, and gets flung into the motor
                compartment where it attracts dust and forms an abrasive paste.
                Some owners also report the belt slipping under load because the
                surplus reduces grip against the front roller. Wipe up any excess
                and use less next time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do all treadmills need lubricating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most do, but not all. A minority ship with pre-waxed or
                self-lubricating decks that must never have silicone applied,
                because adding it to a waxed surface produces a slurry that
                raises friction rather than lowering it. Your manual is the
                authority. If you cannot find it, the manufacturer's support line
                will tell you in under a minute.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Lubricating</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Four errors account for nearly every lubrication job that makes a machine worse rather than better.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reaching for WD-40</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">It is a penetrant, not a lubricant for a sliding polymer interface: it attacks the belt backing and deck laminate, attracts dust, and is flammable near a warm motor. Aerosol "silicone lubricants" in a petroleum carrier are the same trap for careful people. Read the label and use only 100% silicone.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using half a bottle instead of an ounce</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">One ounce across a 20 by 60-inch deck looks like far too little and is exactly right. Excess runs off onto the floor, coats the side rails as a slip hazard, and gets flung into the motor compartment where it binds dust into an abrasive paste. If silicone is pooling at the deck edge you have used several times too much.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying it at the edges of the deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Silicone put near the belt edges migrates straight off the side and never reaches the contact patch that matters. Your feet land in the centre third, and that is where the friction is. Reach in toward the middle of the deck and apply along the centre line, then walk at 2 mph for three to five minutes to spread it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not checking whether the deck is pre-waxed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A minority of decks are waxed or self-lubricating, and silicone on those forms a slurry that raises friction instead of lowering it — the machine gets worse after maintenance. Check the manual before the first lubrication on any machine, or call the manufacturer with the model number. It takes a minute and prevents an expensive confusion.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

